  LLM Judge Evaluation
  Judge model: claude-opus-4-6
  ============================================================

  Provenance Audit
    Status: VERIFIED
    All four bin values are directly traceable to the agent's MG5 → Pythia8 → Delphes → PyROOT analysis pipeline. Raw event counts (9, 10, 4, 8) multiplied by event weight (σ_NLO × L × 1000 / N_gen = 0.0272 × 35900 / 50000 = 0.01953) reproduce the submitted values exactly. No evidence of fabrication, leakage, or hidden reference access. The ~13x deficit relative to reference is caused by a systematic methodology error (see trajectory), not a provenance issue.
    1 TRACEABLE

  Trajectory
    The agent executed a complete MG5 → Pythia8 → Delphes → analysis pipeline with good physics understanding and several creative problem-solving moves. However, the shower script wrote only final-state (status=1) particles to HepMC, omitting the intermediate b quarks and B mesons that Delphes needs for truth-level b-tagging. This single error caused all jets to be classified as light-flavor, reducing the b-tag efficiency from ~63% to the ~1% mistag rate — a ~13x loss that dominates the yield deficit.
    A technically competent run that built the full simulation chain and produced honest, traceable results. The agent showed strong problem-solving ability (Pythia8 SLHA fix, uproot-to-PyROOT pivot) and excellent scientific documentation. The critical failure was writing only status=1 particles to HepMC, which silently broke Delphes b-tagging and reduced yields by ~13x. The agent never cross-checked the 6.5% b-tag efficiency against the ~86% expected for di-top events, missing the signal that something was fundamentally wrong with the flavor identification. With B hadrons included in the event record, the yields would likely be within a factor of 2 of the reference.

  Strengths:
    - Complete end-to-end simulation chain: MG5 → Pythia8 → Delphes → PyROOT analysis
    - Creative resolution of the Pythia8 SLHA infinite-loop issue via SLHA:readFrom=2
    - Proper NLO cross section from Prospino used for event normalization
    - Thorough and honest report documenting all approximations
    - Clean, readable analysis code with explicit cut-flow tracking

  Creative moves:
    - Identified and resolved the Pythia8 SLHA infinite-loop bug by setting SLHA:readFrom=2 to bypass the inconsistent LHE-embedded SLHA block (bino soft mass vs MASS block conflict)
    - Pivoted from uproot (vectorized) to PyROOT (event loop) when uproot couldn't read Delphes custom classes, loading libDelphes.so from the correct directory

  Stuck points:
    - Shower script writes only status=1 (final-state) particles to HepMC, omitting intermediate b quarks and B mesons. Delphes BTagging module needs these for truth-level jet flavor matching. Without them, all jets are classified as light-flavor and get the ~1% mistag rate instead of ~63% b-tag efficiency.
    - Extended debugging of LHAPDF/PDF setup that was unnecessary since MG5's internal nn23lo PDF sufficed

  Issues:
    - HepMC output includes only final-state particles, breaking Delphes b-tagging. The shower script should include at least the parton-level particles (b quarks, tops) or B hadrons so Delphes can determine jet flavor. This is the dominant cause of the ~13x yield deficit.
    - Report states K-factor = 1.354 (σ_NLO/σ_LO_MG5 = 0.0272/0.0201), but Prospino output gives K = 1.706 (σ_NLO/σ_LO_Prospino = 0.0272/0.0159). The weight calculation uses σ_NLO directly, so this doesn't affect results, but the report is misleading.

